{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "A Day With Vanessa", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Talking About Work", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 6, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 16, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Use expressions about relationships and feelings in a range of social and work contexts. ", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations.", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Subject and Object Relative Clauses", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "When you want to clarify which person or thing you are talking about, you use a relative clause.<br/><br/>You can begin a relative clause with <span class=\"italic\">who</span> for people, <span class=\"italic\">which</span> for things or <span class=\"italic\">that</span> for either people or things.<br/><br/>In spoken English you usually use <span class=\"italic\">that</span>, rather than <span class=\"italic\">which</span> or <span class=\"italic\">who</span>.<br/><br/>You put the relative clause immediately after the noun it talks about.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "A subject relative clause describes the subject of the clause.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Example</span>:<br/><span class=\"italic\">The man <span class=\"strong\">who/that</span> called wanted to speak to Tom.</span><br/>Which man? The man who called.<br/>The clause <span class=\"italic\">who called</span> immediately follows <span class=\"italic\">man</span> - the noun it describes.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "An object relative clause describes the object of the clause.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Example</span>:<br/><span class=\"italic\">The book <span class=\"strong\">which/that</span> I lent you was my father's.</span><br/>Which book? The book I lent you.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Native speakers of English often omit the pronoun in object clauses. <br/>For example:<br/>The book I lent you was my father's.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The woman who is riding the bike is my boss.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The man that's sitting on the bench is my friend.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "There's a great view of the park which I love.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Talking About the Future - If, When, Before and After", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use time clauses to give information about time and sequence.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use the words If, When, Before and After in time clauses.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use the first conditional with these time clauses.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "If/When/Before/After + Subject + Simple Present tense, Subject + will/be going to + Simple Present tense", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Before</span> means at an earlier time.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">After</span> means at a later time.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">Before I go to sleep, I'll set my alarm.<br/>After I set my alarm, I'm going to go to sleep.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">If</span> means on the condition that ...", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">If I go to sleep, I'll set my alarm.</span> (I'm not sure that I'll go to sleep.)", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">When</span> means at the time of doing something.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">When I go to bed, I'll set my alarm.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": " I'll do some research about it when I get home.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Before you do that, let's try to guess! ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If they have a miniature horse sculpture in the gift shop, I'm going to buy it! ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "After this, are we going to go for lunch? ", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to hack", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to deal with something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I can't hack seeing her right now.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le16ob06aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about jobs or work.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "the coast is clear", "type": "name"}, {"content": "there is no danger of being seen", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Ask him if the coast is clear. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le16ob06aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to give the all clear", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to announce when a dangerous situation is over", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: After he gives me the all clear, then I'll head to my desk.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le16ob06aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "fear", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the state of being afraid", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Sometimes with respect comes a little fear ...", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le16ob06aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "terror", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a person who inspires great fear", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I don't want to be a terror! ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le16ob06aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "dreadful", "type": "name"}, {"content": "horrible; terrible", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: That's got to be dreadful for the staff. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le16ob06aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"content": "engenn7le16ob06re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Think about what kind of boss you want to have.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Brainstorm some ways to describe a great boss.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Make a list of things that make a boss great to work for.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Compare your l